---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TokenTable from "../components/api/TokenTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "styles", label: "Styles" },
  { id: "sizes", label: "Sizes" },
  { id: "width", label: "Width" },
  { id: "shape", label: "Shape" },
  { id: "disabled", label: "Disabled" },
  { id: "links", label: "As a link" },
  { id: "copy", label: "Copy button" },
  { id: "tokens", label: "Tokens" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="icon_buttons">
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          An icon button is one action shown as one icon, such as a toolbar
          overflow, a dialog's close button, or the star on a row. Add
          <code>icon-button</code> to a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, or to an
          <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> when it navigates, and put a single
          <code>&lt;span class="material-symbols"&gt;</code> inside it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The icon is decoration, so it is <code>aria-hidden</code>, which
          leaves the control without an accessible name. Every icon button
          needs an <code>aria-label</code>.
          <a href="https://github.com/BaezFJ/ExpressiveCSS/blob/master/SEMANTICS.md">SEMANTICS.md</a>
          enforces both rules against the docs, and a bare icon button fails
          the suite.
        </p>
        <p>
          It keeps its own look inside the components that host actions: a
          toolbar, an app bar, a list row, a dialog header, a snackbar, a
          navigation rail, a pane header, or a footer. Those hosts style plain
          controls placed in them and leave a control that styles itself
          alone, as they already do for <code>button</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a separate component, not a modifier on the common
          button. Material 3 gives it its own token families, its own size
          scale and its own colours, and none of them line up with a common
          button's. The older <code>&lt;button class="circle"&gt;</code> still
          works and is still the 40dp common button in a round shape. See
          <a href={route('buttons')}>Buttons</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button" aria-label="Add">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button filled" aria-label="Add">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button tonal" aria-label="Add">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button outlined" aria-label="Add">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
        </p>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" class="icon-button" aria-label="Add">
  <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
</button>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.styles}>
        <p>
          There are four styles, and they use the same modifier names as buttons:
          <strong>standard</strong> is the default and has no container,
          <code>filled</code> is <code>primary</code>,
          <code>tonal</code> is <code>secondary-container</code>, and
          <code>outlined</code> is an <code>outline-variant</code> border with
          no fill. Material 3 gives icon buttons no elevated style, so there is
          no <code>elevated</code> here. Standard is already what
          <code>text</code> would be.
        </p>
        <div class="docs-table-scroll">
        <table class="striped">
          <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Container</th><th>Icon</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>standard (default)</td><td>none</td><td><code>on-surface-variant</code></td><td>The usual case, in toolbars, list rows and app bars.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>filled</code></td><td><code>primary</code></td><td><code>on-primary</code></td><td>The one high-emphasis action on the screen.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tonal</code></td><td><code>secondary-container</code></td><td><code>on-secondary-container</code></td><td>Medium emphasis, without the weight of a filled one.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>outlined</code></td><td>none, <code>outline-variant</code> border</td><td><code>on-surface-variant</code></td><td>Medium emphasis on a busy or image background.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" class="icon-button" aria-label="Add">…</button>
<button type="button" class="icon-button filled" aria-label="Add">…</button>
<button type="button" class="icon-button tonal" aria-label="Add">…</button>
<button type="button" class="icon-button outlined" aria-label="Add">…</button>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.sizes}>
        <p>
          There are five sizes. <code>small</code> is the default at 40dp with a 24dp
          icon, so it needs no class. The rest are
          <code>xsmall</code> (32dp, 20dp icon),
          <code>medium</code> (56dp, 24dp),
          <code>large</code> (96dp, 32dp) and
          <code>xlarge</code> (136dp, 40dp). They compose with the styles:
          <code>class="icon-button tonal large"</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button filled xsmall" aria-label="Add, extra small">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button filled" aria-label="Add, small">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button filled medium" aria-label="Add, medium">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button filled large" aria-label="Add, large">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button filled xlarge" aria-label="Add, extra large">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
        </p>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" class="icon-button filled xsmall" aria-label="Add">…</button>
<button type="button" class="icon-button filled" aria-label="Add">…</button>
<button type="button" class="icon-button filled medium" aria-label="Add">…</button>
<button type="button" class="icon-button filled large" aria-label="Add">…</button>
<button type="button" class="icon-button filled xlarge" aria-label="Add">…</button>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.width}>
        <p>
          Width is the inset plus the icon, so it has no size of its own. That
          is why the default width of every size equals its height, and why a
          round icon button is already a circle. Add <code>narrow</code> to
          tighten the inset or <code>wide</code> to widen it. Both scale with
          whichever size they are on.
        </p>
        <p>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button tonal medium narrow" aria-label="Star, narrow">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">star</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button tonal medium" aria-label="Star, default width">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">star</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button tonal medium wide" aria-label="Star, wide">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">star</span>
          </button>
        </p>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" class="icon-button tonal medium narrow" aria-label="Star">…</button>
<button type="button" class="icon-button tonal medium wide" aria-label="Star">…</button>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.shape}>
        <p>
          Icon buttons are round by default. Add <code>square</code> for the
          squared container, whose corner grows with the size: 12dp on the
          two small sizes, 16dp on medium, and 28dp on the two large ones. Either way the corner tightens
          while the button is pressed, which is the Material 3 Expressive shape
          morph.
        </p>
        <p>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button filled square" aria-label="Edit, square">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">edit</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button filled medium square" aria-label="Edit, medium square">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">edit</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button filled large square" aria-label="Edit, large square">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">edit</span>
          </button>
        </p>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" class="icon-button filled square" aria-label="Edit">…</button>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.disabled}>
        <p>
          Set <code>disabled</code> on the <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>. The icon
          drops to <code>on-surface</code> at 38%, and a style that has a
          container drops it to 10%.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>An <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> cannot be disabled, and no
          class makes it so.</strong> <code>pointer-events: none</code> only
          blocks the pointer. The link stays in the tab order and still
          navigates on Enter, so a <code>.disabled</code> anchor looks inert but
          still works. Remove the <code>href</code>, since an anchor without one
          is neither focusable nor actionable, or remove the link entirely.
          <code>aria-disabled="true"</code> gets the same styling, for an
          element that must stay in the document and announce itself as
          unavailable.
        </p>
        <p>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button" aria-label="Add" disabled>
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button filled" aria-label="Add" disabled>
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button tonal" aria-label="Add" disabled>
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button outlined" aria-label="Add" disabled>
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
          </button>
        </p>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" class="icon-button filled" aria-label="Add" disabled>
  <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">add</span>
</button>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.links}>
        <p>
          An icon button that navigates is an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>. It
          takes <code>icon-button</code> alone. Do not add <code>button</code>,
          which is the common button's class.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="icon-button outlined" href={route('buttons')} aria-label="Open the Buttons page">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">open_in_new</span>
          </a>
        </p>
<Code code={`
<a class="icon-button outlined" href="/buttons" aria-label="Open the Buttons page">
  <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">open_in_new</span>
</a>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.copy}>
        <p>
          Any button copies to the clipboard. <code>data-copy-for</code> names
          the id of a field, which copies its value, or of another element,
          which copies its trimmed text. <code>data-copy</code> holds the text
          itself. A snackbar confirms the result, and screen readers announce
          it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>data-copy-message</code> and <code>data-copy-error</code>
          replace the snackbar's texts for one button, and
          <code>Expressive.Copy.i18n</code> replaces them everywhere. The root
          bundle starts the behavior on load; with the modular entry, call
          <code>Copy.Init()</code>. The clipboard needs HTTPS or localhost;
          anywhere else the snackbar reports the failure.
        </p>
        <div class="field mb-3" style="max-width: 20rem;">
          <input id="copy-demo-key" type="text" placeholder=" " value="sk-live-4f9a2c" readonly>
          <label for="copy-demo-key">API key</label>
          <button type="button" class="icon-button suffix" data-copy-for="copy-demo-key" aria-label="Copy API key">
            <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">content_copy</span>
          </button>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<input id="api-key" value="sk-123" readonly aria-label="API key">
<button type="button" class="icon-button" data-copy-for="api-key" aria-label="Copy API key">
  <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">content_copy</span>
</button>

<button type="button" class="button text" data-copy="https://example.com/share/42"
        data-copy-message="Link copied">Copy link</button>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.tokens}>
        <p>
          Set these on the control, or on an ancestor to reach a group of them.
          Each size class only sets these values, so overriding them gives you
          a custom size.
        </p>
        <TokenTable>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-container-height</code></td><td><code>40px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-icon-size</code></td><td><code>24px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-leading-space</code></td><td><code>8px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-trailing-space</code></td><td><code>8px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-narrow-space</code></td><td><code>4px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-wide-space</code></td><td><code>14px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-container-shape</code></td><td><code>9999px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-container-shape-square</code></td><td><code>12px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-pressed-container-shape</code></td><td><code>8px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-outline-width</code></td><td><code>1px</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-color</code></td><td><code>var(--md-sys-color-on-surface-variant)</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>--md-comp-icon-button-container-color</code></td><td><code>transparent</code></td></tr>
        </TokenTable>
        <p>
          The state layer is always the icon colour mixed into the container at
          the <a href={route('state_layers')}>state layer</a> opacity,
          so those two colour tokens are all a new style needs.
        </p>
  </Section></PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
